## Pages (Workspace Documentation)

**Pages are the canonical store for project documentation in this workspace:**
specifications, design docs, architecture notes, ADRs, runbooks, onboarding
guides, meeting notes, and other long-lived knowledge. They are Markdown
documents organized as a per-workspace tree, with immutable revision
history, per-page ACLs, attachments, and labels.

Agent guidance:
- **Read first.** Before writing new docs or guessing at requirements,
  search the workspace's pages (`ws page list`, optionally filtered by
  `--label spec`/`--label design`) — the specification or runbook may
  already exist.
- **Publish there, not in the repo.** When you produce documentation that
  should be discoverable by the team or by other agents (a spec, a design
  note, a postmortem), create it as a page via `ws page create` instead of
  committing a Markdown file to the repository.
- **Link from work items.** Attach the spec/design page to the work item
  it describes using the "Page" link type (see *Work Items ↔ Pages* below)
  so the relationship is discoverable from both sides.

```bash
# Read
ws page list                                # List non-archived pages
ws page list --label design,spec            # Filter by one or more labels
ws page get <id>                            # Stream page body as Markdown
ws page get <id> -o json                    # Page + metadata as JSON
ws page history <id>                        # List revisions

# Write
ws page create --title "Runbook" --file runbook.md
ws page create --file notes.md --parent <id>          # Place under a parent
ws page create --file blog.md --upload-assets         # Upload local images
ws page edit <id> --file rewritten.md
ws page edit <id> --content ""                       # Intentionally clear body
ws page edit <id> --file blog.md --upload-assets

# Organize
ws page move <id> --parent <id>             # Reparent
ws page move <id> --root                    # Move to workspace root
ws page move <id> --parent <id> --after <sibling-id>   # Insert after sibling
ws page move <id> --parent <id> --before <sibling-id>  # Insert before sibling
ws page archive <id>                        # Archive page + subtree

# Labels
ws page-label list                          # List workspace labels
ws page-label create --name design --color "#0ea5e9"
ws page-label labels <page-id>              # Show / attach / detach / replace
```

Notes:
- `--upload-assets` scans Markdown for `![alt](./local.png)` refs, uploads each
  local image as a page attachment, and rewrites the link to the hosted URL.
- `ws page get` defaults to streaming Markdown regardless of the global `-o`.

## Work Items ↔ Pages

Work items track *what to do*; pages hold the *documentation that describes it*
— the spec, the design, the runbook, the postmortem. The two are cross-linked
through the built-in **"Page"** link type (entity types `["item","page"]`).
Use a link whenever an item has an authoritative spec or design page so that
either side surfaces the other. Both sides must share a workspace; per-page
ACLs are honored when listing links.

- A work item's detail view shows a **Pages** section alongside Linked items.
- A page's detail view shows a **Work items** popover with linking items.
- API endpoints: `GET /items/{id}/links`, `GET /pages/{id}/links`,
  `POST /item-links`, plus `GET /workspaces/{id}/pages/search?q=` for picker UIs.
- The ws CLI does not yet expose `link`-management subcommands; use the
  REST API (the agent token's `items:write` + `pages:read` scopes cover it)
  or the web UI to add or remove links.

## Scopes & Capabilities

`ws init` mints a token with the default agent scope set. Capabilities:

| Area | Scopes | Capability |
|------|--------|------------|
| Work items | `items:read`, `items:write` | List, get, create, edit, move, set milestones. **Delete is not granted by default.** |
| Workspaces | `workspaces:read`, `workspaces:write` | Discover and configure workspaces. |
| Users | `users:read` | Look up users for assignments / mentions. |
| Item taxonomy | `item-types:read`, `workflows:read`, `statuses:read`, `priorities:read`, `custom-fields:read` | Read-only — enough to validate transitions and resolve names. |
| Planning | `milestones:read`, `iterations:read`, `projects:read` | Read-only. Mint with explicit scopes to write to these. |
| Pages | `pages:read`, `pages:write`, `pages:delete` | Full lifecycle: read, create, edit, move, label, archive. Per-page ACLs still apply in-handler. |
| MCP | `mcp:access` | Use Windshift's MCP server (single binary scope — exposes both read and write tools). |

Excluded from the default mint (require an explicit `--scopes` / permissions list):
- `items:delete`
- `milestones:write|delete`, `iterations:write|delete`, `projects:write|delete`
- `actions:read|write` (automation graphs)
- `collections:read`
- All `admin:*` scopes (also require the system admin role on the user)

## Test Management

```bash
# Test Cases
ws test case ls                    # List all test cases
ws test case get <id>              # Get case with steps

# Test Runs
ws test run mine                   # My assigned runs
ws test run ls                     # List all runs
ws test run get <id>               # Get run with results
ws test run start <set-id>         # Start new run from set
ws test run end <id>               # End/complete a run

# Recording Results
ws test result <run-id> <case-id> passed
ws test result <run-id> <case-id> failed --notes "Issue description"
```

## Configuration

Project config is stored in `ws.toml`. Global config is at `~/.config/ws/config.toml`.

```bash
ws config show                     # Show effective config
ws config init                     # Initialize config
```
